Strategies of the Automatic Players:
Naive Player:
The strategy of the naive player was to iterate through all rows,
and for each row through all columns.
If it finds an empty cell, it places its mark there and returns.
This player simply takes the first available spot on the board.

Whatever Player:
This player’s strategy is based on randomness.
It randomly selects a row and a column within
the board’s limits and, if the cell is empty,
places its mark there.
This represents a completely unpredictable and
unplanned style of play.

Smart Player:
The smart player’s strategy takes advantage of the fact that both the
naive and the whatever players follow deterministic and predictable behaviors.
We know exactly how the naive player behaves-
it always goes row by row and fills the first available empty cell.
The whatever player acts randomly and does not use any structured pattern.
To consistently win more than 80% of the games,
especially on the default board size (4×4) with a winning streak of 3,
the smart player focuses on column priority.
It starts by playing in the second column (index 2)
and moves downward in that column.
For larger boards or different win-streak settings,
the smart player continues filling one entire column before
moving to the next column.
This strategy ensures that the smart player always plays in structured,
central areas of the board, maximizing it's chance to form winning
streaks while keeping the algorithm simple and predictable.
Naturally, the player only places a mark in a cell if it is empty.

Advantage of Using a Common Interface:

The advantage of designing the software so that all player classes implement the
same interface is that it allows the game to interact with all types of players
in the same way.
The main program doesn’t need to know which specific kind of player it is
dealing with- it only requires that the player knows how to make a move.

This design relies on important object-oriented programming principles:
it demonstrates polymorphism, by allowing many different implementations to
share a single interface, and it also applies abstraction,
by separating the what from the how.
In other words, the code knows what it needs (a player that can play),
but not how each player actually performs their moves- those details are
handled in each specific class.

This approach makes the system easier to extend and maintain,
since new types of players can be added without changing the
game’s main logic (by the factory)